Thanks, Omer. I like the direction here, generally.
 
I want us to enable teams like DI&D to build and innovate quickly in support of client delivery without requiring IT to be in the middle of every development decision. At the same time, we need a consistent enterprise boundary around client data, identity/access, approved platforms and models, security, auditability, and anything that moves into repeatable or production use.
 
You also shouldn't lose sight of the lifecycle side of this. Development is only part of the cost. Anything you put into production eventually requires patching, maintenance, monitoring, support, and ownership when something breaks or needs to evolve. You also need to avoid creating key-person dependencies where one individual holds all of the development knowledge for a solution. Documentation, source control, knowledge sharing, and appropriate backup ownership need to be part of the model from the outset.
 
If we can establish that controlled environment and make the “paved road” easy to use, I think we can accomplish both objectives: distributed innovation and development, with centralized guardrails and governance.
